 \section{What the results show and what they do not}\label{sec:discussion}
 % BEGIN-EV2
-\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} Against the identity-replacement attacker, targeting new identities lowers the gain that free replacement buys: R3 leaves a lower gain than uniform auditing on every sampled market, with its efficiency condition met. The margin the first test claimed does not carry over to the whole neighbourhood, and the advantage is not specific to R3: a rule that ranks identities by verified history does about as well, and in the factorial either the priority phase or newest-first targeting alone recovers most of the effect. Within the scope of Section~\ref{sec:design}, the finding therefore holds across the tested age-ranked variants and the selected verified-history comparator; it is not specific to R3's particular design. Pacing contributes little at the registered audit cost ($\rho = \const{0.2}$), and the terminal reserve that pacing adds is the source of R3's collapse at high audit cost ($\rho = \const{1}$), which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
+\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, with R3's registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule's frozen truthful reference; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities, which would need a matched comparison with replacement disabled. The margin the first test claimed does not carry over to the whole neighbourhood. The pooled factorial contrasts and the comparison with C$^*$ support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do not establish the same ordering for every variant on every market. At $\rho = \const{0.2}$, pacing slightly raises the measured gain while changing efficiency little; at $\rho = \const{1}$, the terminal reserve that pacing adds exhausts the allowance and causes R3's collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
 
