 \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
 % BEGIN-EV2
-Free identity replacement makes a ban costless under uniform audits, and in our tests directing audits at new or unverified identities reduces the measured replacement gain. Two registered tests, the second on fresh markets drawn after the lock, show that this holds in sign across the sampled markets but not with the margin first claimed, that a verified-history rule matches the age-ranked rule we proposed, and that neither protects efficiency against loss-accepting welfare attacks. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, is released with the paper.
+For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the registered margin under both Q1 families. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks reduce the gross efficiency of every core rule while earning less than the truthful reference, and a post hoc lottery shows that the profit-constrained welfare result depends on excluding randomised policies. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.
