H4's code was committed only by its hash before the lock and released after the report review; it is in \texttt{sealed2/h4/} of the release (bundle SHA-256 \texttt{cba512aa\ldots}), with its author's note on which families it contains as special cases.

\paragraph{Search quality.} Scheduled cells get a doubling check: the registered search continues with as many restarts again, and the cell is flagged if the doubled search improves the attacker's normalised profit or the reference's normalised profit (profit cells), the welfare attack's efficiency (welfare cells), or the coalition objective by at least \const{0.005}, or if a W1 search finds a feasible candidate where the original found none. A doubled search's winner is reported beside the cell and never replaces its frozen estimate. Cells without a scheduled check carry no search-quality claim. A cell is \emph{missing} if it did not complete or is invalid: not run (a cap stop or a cut), a refused sample, a nonfinite statistic, a search with no finite candidate, or a failed load of H4; a missing cell removes every decision that uses it. A \emph{search-flagged} cell keeps its decision, labelled as a decision about the prescribed search procedure, and leaves that rule's robustness statement for that family unanswered. The search bounds exposure to the searched families and optimiser; it does not certify robustness.

\section{The first test (October 2026)}\label{sec:eval1}
\paragraph{Claims.} The original claim was locked and timestamped on 17 October 2026, before the rule was implemented. At $\beta = \evone{0.05}$, the median position, and on 40 fresh seeds, its \emph{rule clause} required, for each confirmatory family, a one-sided 95\% lower bound of R3's gross efficiency of at least \evone{0.90}, a one-sided 95\% upper bound of its gain of at most \evone{0.30}, and audit cost within budget on every seed. Its \emph{baseline clause} required, for each of B2 and B3, a confirmatory family in which either the paired inflation-caused efficiency-loss difference exceeded \evone{0.05} or the baseline's gain exceeded \evone{1.0}, at the 98.75\% level. Amendment 1, registered after the baselines' development results showed that the baseline clause was unlikely to pass, added an amended claim on the public identity-replacement family D2+H3: the same absolute part as the rule clause (gross-efficiency lower bound at least \evone{0.90}, gain upper bound at most \evone{0.30}, budget compliance) and paired gain reductions of at least \evone{0.10} against B2 and B3 at the 98.75\% level, plus the H2 rule clause. After a development test killed the first rule, Amendment 3 registered one successor, R3, before its implementation.

\paragraph{Sealing.} The sealed family H2 was written in a separate session from a pinned copy of the repository, and only its hash was committed. Before unsealing, at the author's request, the custodian disclosed that H2 contains D2; that H2 does not exit or re-enter was stated in the public specification before the lock.

